      #article
        section.wrapper-padding.article-section(id="mission")
          h2.heading-underline The mission & first day
          .explanation-wrapper
            div
              p.summary-text
                em The mission: 
                | Change a creation tool to be used by a different audience. For example, making Photoshop beginner friendly. 
              p.summary-text
                em The first day: 
                | We collectively wanted to focus on accessibility in this competition. Opportunity arose when we used a keyboard to navigate around our brainstorming whiteboard. It was so unreliable tabbing through content.
              p.summary-text
                em That got us thinking: 
                | how do blind and low vision (
                abbr(aria-label="Blind and Low Vision") BLV
                | ) users use these visual-based whiteboarding tools?
            .trailer.subheading
              h3.heading-underline Early discovery phase: Keyboard navigation with FigJam
              video(
                  width="1600" 
                  height="1064"
                  autoplay
                  muted
                  loop
                  controls
                  )
                  source(src="../../video/design/adaptive-interfaces/navigating-figjam.mp4" type="video/mp4")
        section.wrapper-padding.article-section(id="research")
          h2.heading-underline Research
          .explanation-wrapper
            div
              p
                | Secondary research pointed to how cognitively demanding it was for  
                abbr(aria-label="Blind and Low Vision") BLV
                |  users: they’re in a team discussion, navigating through the board with a screen reader, and contributing to the board. All simultaneously.
              p
                | We hosted a mock event planning meeting using Miro. I used a screen reader and a towel over my monitor and documented my experience using Miro while visually deprived.
              p
                | While our simulation is not in any way representative of how blind users might use Miro and screen readers, the exercise helped me gain empathy more than anything. We read the papers, but experiencing it first-hand made me realize:
              p
                | oh, this is a 
                em big 
                | problem.
              
            .subheading.multiple-media
              div
                h3.heading-underline From a 
                  a(href="https://dl.acm.org/doi/epdf/10.1145/2700648.2809864#page=5" target=_blank) workplace accessibility field study.
                div.content-border.pullquote
                  p “One thing I learned when I was working with sighted people is they would talk while I was still listening [to my screen reader]… I can’t listen to two streams at the same time."
                  p - Catherine

              div
                h3.heading-underline My crude method to restrict my senses
                img(
                  alt="Winner-takes-all system" 
                  width="1600" 
                  height="1200" 
                  src="../../img/design/adaptive-interfaces/autoethnography.webp"
                  )
        section.wrapper-padding.article-section(id="solution")
          h2.heading-underline Our solution
          div.section-spacing
            div
              p
                | We created an accessibility onboarding experience for first-time 
                abbr(aria-label="Blind and Low Vision") BLV
                |  Miro users with a focus on toolbar customization. The user can start with a simple 6-tool toolbar to gradually learn Miro and then create their own custom toolbar aligned with their needs.
            .trailer.subheading
              h3.heading-underline Adaptive Interfaces Prototype
              video(
                  width="1544" 
                  height="1080"
                  muted
                  controls
                  )
                  source(src="../../video/design/adaptive-interfaces/prototype-walkthrough.mp4" type="video/mp4")
              a.button-small(
                target="_blank" 
                href="https://bluejunimo.github.io/SparkJamMiroExhibition/"
                ) Visit website
          .explanation-wrapper
            div
              p
                | We agreed that tackling this big problem in a small way would help us design something feasible and grounded in research. We leaned into customization to reduce cognitive overload of learning so many tools and to accommodate the differing needs of 
                abbr(aria-label="Blind and Low Vision") BLV
                |    users.
              
            .subheading
              div
                h3.heading-underline From a 
                  a(href="https://faculty.washington.edu/wobbrock/pubs/w4a-25.02.pdf#:~:text=%E2%80%9CBeing,%E2%80%9D" target=_blank) screen reader study.
                div.content-border.pullquote
                  p “Being able to choose the information felt important and meaningful because I had more autonomy and control over the information I wanted; it would save time not having to listen to information I didn’t need or care about." 
                  p - Participant C2
        section.wrapper-padding.article-section(id="prototyping")
          h2.heading-underline Prototyping with AI
          .explanation-wrapper.section-spacing
            div
              p
                | I used AI (Claude Code) to prototype our website as a Figma prototype would limit our ability to showcase how it would be used by a screen reader.
              p 
                | I designed high-fidelity static pages in Figma and sent them to Claude with instructions on the interaction design. Iterations on ARIA use, alt-text, and focus order were made to align the prototype to how our target audience would use Miro.
          .explanation-wrapper.two-sections.media-only
            div
              .subheading
                h3.heading-underline Screen reader accessibility iterations 
                .dual-video-wrapper
                  .dual-video-buttons
                    a.button-small(
                      href="#" 
                      data-src="../../video/design/adaptive-interfaces/prototype-before-accessibility-iterations.mp4"
                      )
                      svg(xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="48" height="48" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" aria-hidden="false" alt-text="Play").play-pause-icon
                        path(d="m380-300 280-180-280-180v360ZM480-80q-83 0-156-31.5T197-197q-54-54-85.5-127T80-480q0-83 31.5-156T197-763q54-54 127-85.5T480-880q83 0 156 31.5T763-763q54 54 85.5 127T880-480q0 83-31.5 156T763-197q-54 54-127 85.5T480-80Zm0-80q134 0 227-93t93-227q0-134-93-227t-227-93q-134 0-227 93t-93 227q0 134 93 227t227 93Zm0-320Z")
                      | Screen reader before accessibility iterations 
                    a.button-small(
                      href="#" 
                      data-src="../../video/design/adaptive-interfaces/prototype-after-accessibility-iterations.mp4"
                      )
                      svg(xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="48" height="48" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" aria-hidden="false" alt-text="Play").play-pause-icon
                        path(d="m380-300 280-180-280-180v360ZM480-80q-83 0-156-31.5T197-197q-54-54-85.5-127T80-480q0-83 31.5-156T197-763q54-54 127-85.5T480-880q83 0 156 31.5T763-763q54 54 85.5 127T880-480q0 83-31.5 156T763-197q-54 54-127 85.5T480-80Zm0-80q134 0 227-93t93-227q0-134-93-227t-227-93q-134 0-227 93t-93 227q0 134 93 227t227 93Zm0-320Z")
                      | Screen reader after accessibility iterations 
                  video(
                    id="dual_video_player"
                    width="1600" 
                    height="1064"
                    controls
                    playsinline
                    )
                    source(src="../../video/design/adaptive-interfaces/prototype-before-accessibility-iterations.mp4" type="video/mp4")
                   
        section.wrapper-padding.article-section(id="storytelling")
          h2.heading-underline Storytelling (feat. Linda)
          .explanation-wrapper
            div
              p I drafted a persona right after our research concluded. We used Linda in mentor critiques to give backstory on the downsides of Miro and how our proposed solutions address those pain points. She was also effective in 
                a(href="#check_it_out") our video pitch.
            .subheading
              h3.heading-underline Linda kicks off our pitch video
              img(
                  alt="User persona card for 'Linda', a Backend Software Developer, illustrated with an orange avatar wearing sunglasses. Background: Linda works in an agile team of five on fraud systems backend and struggles to participate in virtual scrum meetings using Miro, which limits her ability to work with teammates and stifles her professional development. What she's tried so far: her PM and team accommodate her by following Miro's accessibility best practices and verbally describing what they reference on the board. Frustrations: writing down her thoughts is time consuming; relying on a teammate to write for her; navigating the interface is unreliable with a screen reader; inaccurately positioning elements on the board. Her needs: to easily collaborate with her team during brainstorming sessions, and to comprehend teammates' contributions so she can stay informed and meaningfully contribute." 
                  width="2600" 
                  height="1463" 
                  src="../../img/design/adaptive-interfaces/persona-linda.webp"
                  data-zoomable
                  )
        section#what_i_learned.wrapper-padding.article-section
          h2.heading-underline What I learned
          .explanation-wrapper
            ul.fancy-list
              li Making designs accessible brings more meaning to my work. This is something I want to continue in future work.
              li After vibe coding for the first time, I now better understand AI’s value in design communication: it’s a prototyping tool just like Figma, to be used in certain situations based on your project’s solution. It also lets me spend more time designing and less on prototyping.
              li While our research was lengthy, I don’t imagine how our project would have survived without it. Linda wouldn’t exist and our design decisions would be based off vibes.
